## Purpose

`pi-until` releases the current model turn while a cheap shell predicate waits or a recurring agent task approaches its next fixed-cadence tick. The owner is the current live Pi session. It is not a durable scheduler.

## Lifecycle boundary

- A normal turn can settle without stopping watches.
- `/reload` suspends active watch values to the session and restores them in the replacement extension instance before the original expiry.
- A graceful quit (`session_shutdown { reason: "quit" }`) writes a version 3 suspension entry with `reason` and the owning `sessionId`. The next long-lived process that opens the same session file (`session_start` `startup` or `resume`) restores those watches with no command, then appends `pi-until-resumed` to consume the entry. Expired, finished, duplicate, and already-active watches are skipped. Deadlines and recurring cadence stay anchored to the original start.
- `/new`, fork, a forked copy of a quit entry, a reload entry found at startup, a crash, SIGKILL, and reboot give no authority to restart. Print and JSON runs neither restore nor write a quit entry.
- `/until-resume` remains the manual path when automatic recovery did not apply.
- History: 2026-09-21 (`0.5.0`) made quit write an entry and added `/until-resume` as an explicit path. On 2026-09-22 Joel quit Pi for a runtime update. The drovr hawk session lost three watches because its process had loaded `cf28671` before `0.5.0` reached disk and never reloaded, so the reload-only handler ran at quit and wrote nothing. Even a written entry would have required a command nobody knew about. `0.6.0` makes quit-then-relaunch automatic. Version 2 quit entries left by `0.5.0` processes restore when they are newer than the session header and no message follows them.
- Same-process session replacement creates a fresh session delivery arbiter. A stopped arbiter is never reused.
- Session shutdown writes its suspension synchronously before awaiting restoration, claim settlement, or shell cleanup. Normal shutdown still waits for shell process-tree cleanup before publication.
- SIGHUP and SIGTERM synchronously journal and kill this extension's running shell process groups. A host signal handler gets time to settle its pending claims and ownership checks before publication. Without a host handler, one process-wide registry publishes all instances before re-raising the signal. A synchronous `exit` hook covers the dead-terminal exit 129; 143 is an explicit TERM exit used by RPC/print hosts, not a dead-terminal code. Default signal delivery emits no `exit` event. Unrelated crash exits do not create quit authority.
- After graceful settlement, a newly captured claim or confirmed foreign ownership can append a corrective suspension. The newest entry wins before the single publication. A payload keeps its transfer id across retries. Publication becomes committed as soon as its rename succeeds, even if the subsequent directory fsync fails.
- Emergency exit probes completed claim files and foreign ownership evidence synchronously. A libuv rename still in flight at process exit remains an unrecoverable window. An interrupted reload retains reload authority only, not automatic quit authority. A host that catches a signal and keeps running leaves pi-until stopped until reload.
- Exit persistence errors append only the time, session id and error code to a private log when storage permits. Commands, output, watch definitions and error messages are excluded. Startup maintenance removes the log after the handoff retention period or above 64 KiB.
- The shared signal registry excludes signal-exit v3/v4 emitter counts from host-handler detection. V3 stores its emitter on `process.__signal_exit_emitter__`; v4 stores it on `globalThis[Symbol.for("signal-exit emitter")]`. Those observers wait for other listeners before re-raising; counting them as hosts would swallow signals indefinitely. The registry observes ordinary host handlers before they can remove themselves during disposal. Its shared key and shape are a backward-compatible protocol, not a package-version key.
- A host still alive five seconds after a caught signal produces one local `signal-held` telemetry event per stopped session. `/until-stats` reports that the host held the signal for ≥5 s, not that it necessarily stayed alive indefinitely. Each delayed callback is guarded, and its session id is captured at installation rather than read from a potentially stale context. Teardown cancels the delayed observation; a failed non-quit shutdown still detaches its client.
- The shutdown boundary is claimed before journal I/O. A failed write still cancels actors and kills running checks. Hooks retry from frozen pre-cancellation values only while the context remains live. After a graceful quit with a failed initial or corrective journal, Pi invalidates the context before exit. There is no exit-time recovery guarantee. Inspect shutdown/publication notices and the private error log, then use `/until-resume` for available saved work; no command can restore an unwritten suspension. The stale-context settlement test asserts the logged failure, not publication.
- A waiting successor lists a separate pending inventory: ids, labels, watch expiry, predecessor, and handoff deadline. The fork snapshot is advisory, not permission to start actors. Unknown inventory stays visibly pending while the handoff remains unresolved; an explicit release ends that wait without inventing missing ids.

Pi `0.84.2` supports this boundary through `session_shutdown`, `session_start`, `agent_settled`, `message_start`, `ctx.isIdle()`, custom session entries, and follow-up messages. The compaction hold also needs `session_before_compact`, `session_compact`, and `session_compact_failed`. Pi added `session_compact_failed` in `0.85`, so the dev pins moved to `0.87.1`, the fleet runtime. On older Pi, a failed compaction holds wakes until the next `agent_settled`.

## Predecessor death and handoff release

A missing publication is not proof that the predecessor died. Pi can overwrite its OS-visible argv with its process title, so a process scan with no session-id match cannot authorize re-arming. Older predecessors without identity records retain the existing 30-minute ceiling. At that deadline, missing records produce an explicit release with inventory ids, not an indefinite passive wait. The message says the predecessor never handed off; it does not claim the process died. Unreadable, malformed or unprobeable records are unknown rather than absent and cannot authorize that fallback.

New long-lived sessions atomically write owner-only liveness sidecars under the handoff directory. A sidecar contains only session id, pid and OS process start time. It is evidence in the same host's process namespace, not a portable ownership proof. A successor may classify death only from an existing valid record: the pid is absent, or its current start time differs. A matching start time means alive; any unavailable or inconclusive observation means unknown. Publication removes only the publishing process's record, after the mailbox is visible; stale records are advisory. Pre-feature runtimes cannot refresh them; use the stopped-runtime rollback cleanup below.

The early-release lifecycle is `waiting → death grace (~10 seconds) → verify → released`. An alive or unknown observation resets the grace. At the ceiling, a recorded alive/unknown identity enters watching and continues identity monitoring; later death still takes the grace. Its inventory says a late handoff may still arrive and to re-arm only if the predecessor is gone, not the blanket do-not-re-arm warning. Every claim attempt gives a publication priority; the final check re-observes death and attempts the claim again before releasing. An early release consumes the predecessor binding before inviting manual re-arming. Its notice names the predecessor and advisory inventory ids; no watch is restored from inventory. Reload must not restart that released adoption, and late publication after release must not race newly re-armed work. No daemon, new watch durability, or argv/PID environment protocol is added.

A `pi-until-handoff-deadline` custom entry records the absolute wait deadline. Reload and transitive adoption take the earliest known deadline, never a fresh full window. A publication carrying a pending predecessor writes deadline metadata under `handoff/deadlines/<transfer-id>.json` before the canonical mailbox. The transfer id and predecessor must match at readback. The deadline is journalled before the claim is completed, including interrupted-claim forwarding. These additive records leave the strict existing v3 watch and mailbox payloads unchanged for rollback readers. A legacy payload with no recoverable deadline metadata cannot reveal a lost original deadline. It receives the normal ceiling once, journals that deadline, then releases if still unrecorded. This migration fallback does not permit early release of unknown legacy processes. Every deadline recorded from this version onward is never extended. The legacy empty tombstone still takes the existing `nothing` path and consumes the binding without a release notice. Carried pending suspensions preserve an ancestor's advisory ids even when an outer adopted suspension owns no watches. Consumed pending links are not forwarded into another publication.

## Review-hardened release boundary

Release permission belongs only to the session whose `successorId` stamped it. Forks never inherit it. Same-session reload and partial re-arm retain the release packet and original inventory. Partial re-arm does not identify which old ids remain, so check active watches and handled work before starting more. A new adoption, successor binding or quit ends that view. Its old inventory ids are still removed from future forks' advisory inventories. Release notifications explicitly use `triggerTurn: false`, not steering delivery. A foreign claim/receipt has its own claimed-elsewhere outcome: claimed by another session; do not re-arm. An existing unreadable ownership artifact keeps that conservative restriction and emits an inspection warning. An entry removed between directory listing and reading is not evidence. A final claim attempt gives a publication priority even when foreign evidence was seen. If `until list` shows a pending handoff, an inherited release message does not apply.

Linux identity uses `/proc/<pid>/stat` field 22 plus the boot id; macOS uses the fixed process start time from `ps`. Linux records containing only pre-upgrade wall-clock identity remain unknown rather than being mistaken for pid reuse. Watching probes back off to five seconds outside the death grace. Failed identity registration warns even without an exception. Missing-record deadline releases warn that the predecessor may still be running: confirm it has stopped before re-arming. A later publication is not adopted after release; reopening the old predecessor can restore its watches beside manually re-armed work.

Metadata pruning retains live/unknown identities and counts dead-record retention from the first conclusive prune observation, not process startup mtime. Identity-bound observations under `handoff/liveness-deaths/` preserve long-lived predecessors' records through their death grace; orphan observations expire too. Deletion quarantines and rechecks candidates, restoring refreshed records without replacing newer canonical files. Pruning also removes orphan deadlines after retention and stale temp files. Referenced canonical/claimed transfer deadlines stay. For rollback, first stop candidate-version sessions and their successor waits on this host, then remove their advisory identity files before reopening any session on an older runtime: `find "$HOME/.pi/agent/pi-until/handoff/liveness" -type f -name '*.json' -delete`. This is an operator rollback step, not a worker action or a journal deletion. Do not remove canonical mailboxes, claims, receipts or session journals.

## Two machines

Each watch is an XState v6 actor (`6.0.0-beta.1`). It owns cadence, gate checks, pending delivery, settlement, completion, expiry, cancellation, and typed terminal failure.

One session XState actor owns all `pi-until` delivery. It:

- queues due watches without submitting two follow-ups to Pi at once;
- deduplicates recurring ticks by watch;
- stamps dispatch time before packet rendering;
- correlates `message_start` through `details.followUpId`;
- waits for the resulting `agent_settled` before dispatching the next item;
- pauses in `startUncertain` when acknowledgement takes more than five seconds;
- reconciles a late acknowledgement without changing the packet's cadence facts;
- fails only on explicit dispatch rejection;
- drops queued work whose watch is no longer live;
- holds dispatch while Pi compacts outside an agent run, then waits a five-second grace window so a prompt queued behind compaction starts first.

### Compaction hold

On 2026-09-25 Joel reported that short-interval watches disturbed compaction in the drovr hawk session. Pi `0.87.1` `sendCustomMessage(..., { triggerTurn: true })` starts `_runAgentPrompt` whenever no agent run is active. `prompt()` refuses input during manual compaction, but that path has no guard. Manual compaction first aborts the run and then summarizes outside one, so the arbiter sat in `ready` and dispatched wakes into it. The hawk session has one compaction: extension-provided, at 751,810 tokens on a 1M-window model, far below the threshold, so manual. Wakes ran full tool turns during the summary. When it landed, the bridge tore down the in-flight query, the wake's turn ended as an empty `aborted` assistant, and the next wake started 120 ms later. A real Pi RPC run with a slow faux summarizer reproduced the turn inside `compaction_start`/`compaction_end`. With the hold, the wake arrived five seconds after `compaction_end`.

Compaction inside an agent run needs no hold: threshold, overflow, and between-tool-step compaction happen while the arbiter is already busy. Checks keep running during a hold; only dispatch waits. `ctx.isIdle()` is false while Pi compacts, so starting a watch then must not mark the session busy, or the queue would wait for a turn that never comes. Pre-prompt compaction after an aborted run has the same shape as manual compaction and gets the same hold.

An acknowledgement timeout is uncertainty, not proof that Pi rejected the message. Sending another follow-up at that point could stack agent turns.

## Agent contract

- `start` waits for a side-effect-free shell predicate. Exit code 0 is the only true result.
- Conditions must fail closed. On 2026-09-28 two sessions got false-success wakes on check 1 from conditions that exited 0 on missing input, including an empty script written in the same tool batch that armed the watch, and conditions that read stale output or a dead pid file because the call that reset them ran in the same parallel batch. A marker-line success mode was rejected because it would split the single exit-code gate; `grep -qx MARKER file` already expresses it. The fix was guidance in the README and tool text. An optional first-check delay is pending Joel's decision.
- `repeat` asks the same agent to do work on a fixed cadence. A gate permits a tick but never completes the task.
- `instruction`, `quickRef`, `contextRefs`, and origin are immutable private session data.
- Context references remain opaque until the waking agent needs one.
- Only explicit `complete`, `cancel`, typed failure, or expiry ends a recurrence.
- An expired or failed packet is inactive. The agent does not continue it without a new user request.
- Reading a finished watch with `status` or `cancel` drops only that watch's queued terminal wake; `list` does not consume wakes, and already dispatched wakes still settle normally.

The public contract lives across `README.md`, `src/command.ts`, the registered tool guidance in `extensions/pi-until.ts`, and `src/packet.ts`. `AGENTS.md` names this set so future behavior changes update it together.

## Privacy and bounds

- Shell stdout and stderr never enter receipts or model context.
- Telemetry is local JSONL. It stores a 12-character condition hash, never command fragments, recurring instructions, quick references, or context pointers.
- The extension retains the newest 50 terminal receipts in memory.
- The flat tool schema has one `Type.Object` root for the OpenAI Codex bridge. `prepareArguments` repairs only known string encodings before normal validation.

## Rejected designs

- Global live actors across `/reload`: stale Pi contexts and split cleanup ownership.
- A daemon or reboot scheduler: violates session ownership.
- Per-watch delivery decisions: cannot enforce session-wide serialization.
- Treating acknowledgement timeout as rejection: the accepted message can still start after slow `before_agent_start` hooks.
- Pausing checks during compaction: a check never touches the session, and paused checks would delay the wake without protecting anything.

## Terminal restart evidence (2026-11-11)

A real interactive Pi 1.5.0-p10 under a private PTY reproduced the failure with one inert watch. `ctx.shutdown()` and SIGTERM with a live terminal each wrote one suspension and one mailbox. Closing the PTY master exited 129 without either write. A fixture observer registered after pi-until's shutdown handler was never reached; this does not prove that teardown never began.

The installed interactive runtime handles SIGHUP/SIGTERM, but dead-terminal stream errors call `emergencyTerminalExit()`, which invokes `process.exit(129)` without extension cleanup. The extension runner awaits handlers in order, so an earlier handler or pi-until's own cleanup awaits can yield before the suspension is written. Moving only the session-shutdown write is insufficient; synchronous signal and exit hooks cover that gap.

`tests/live-restart.py` exercises interactive quit, quit during a shell check, PTY-master close, SIGHUP, SIGTERM, and direct emergency exit. Two explicitly targeted successors compete for each handoff. The proof checks one parent suspension, one successor adoption, preserved watch identity/start/expiry, and zero model turns. These common-path cases do not prove settlement races.

Review of the first packet found two settlement regressions: an in-flight claim could be stranded, and a transferred same-session quit could be re-suspended. `tests/restart-settlement.test.ts` holds claim renames before and after their filesystem effect, then checks third-successor adoption. It also covers a transferred reopen interrupted during ownership checking, and publication failure before or after rename. `tests/exit-hooks.test.ts` runs isolated Node processes to prove one signal hook and actual HUP/TERM termination for one or two module copies. It covers bare Node, signal-exit v3/v4 shapes, both shapes together, and the installed signal-exit module when `PI_UNTIL_SIGNAL_EXIT_MODULE` names it. Set `PI_UNTIL_SIGNAL_EXIT_COMPANION_MODULE` as well to cover real v3 and v4 together. `npm run check` now discovers npm's bundled v4 and the repository's v3 automatically; missing modules emit a loud coverage warning. A draining-host test keeps a stale client beside a live one through the five-second observation and successful publication. A watchdog exposes a swallowed real signal instead of allowing natural exit to hide it. A once-only host test proves settlement still precedes publication. Lifecycle tests cover caught signals and emergency exit during reload.

### Handback when an adopted successor is discarded

Do not equate `dropWatches` with handback. It does not say whether work was cancelled or relocated, and a running predecessor may still hold stale state. A safe handback needs an explicit ownership transfer from the current holder to one named recipient, a durable receipt consuming the current holder's work, and the same atomic claim protocol. Muster must name the recipient and distinguish discard from transfer before closing the pane. This is not a small extension-only change, so it remains deferred. Ordinary successor shutdown already forwards its owned watches and any pending predecessor chain.

## XState 6 compatibility

The port from XState `5.32.5` preserves watch lifecycle and persistence, with one internal timing difference: v5 ran actions inline before snapshot commit; v6 commits the snapshot, then runs effects in enqueue order. A port that re-enters and reads the actor sees the new state and context. Its synchronous sends remain mailbox-serialized. For example, dispatch after a compaction-ending settlement now sees `compacting: false`. The re-entrant port regression in `tests/follow-up.test.ts` pins this behavior. Keep eventless `always` bodies, `isLive` and clock reads side-effect free: an enabled eventless function is evaluated during selection and again during execution; ordinary event transitions reuse their selection result. Effects belong behind the enqueuer.

Suspension stores version 3 watch definitions and facts, never XState's internal actor snapshots. The parser, journal writer, handoff format, cadence and expiry anchors remain unchanged. No state migration or new suspension version is needed. State written by the v6 build remains readable by the v5 build at `7fa41bc`.

The first port used `6.0.0-alpha.73` (release tag `2ee15f5a1cd0ad07053afbd47ad5eaf018027e92`). The follow-up pins `6.0.0-beta.1` (tag `d84d1dedac9f2c62082cf2c2094daee8d32ad0a9`), checked against its bundled migration guide, declarations, changelog and tagged TypeScript source. Beta.0 moves raw actor-snapshot restoration to `restoreActor` from `xstate/persist`, rejects legacy config and unknown actor options, changes schema accessors, improves `self` typing and production diagnostics, and rejects obsolete suffixed lifecycle event keys. None of those removed APIs is used here. Beta.1 adds static target checking to `setup().createMachine`; the existing targets typecheck unchanged.

Unlike alpha.73's default 1000-microstep error, beta.1 defaults to unbounded macrosteps, matching v5. Development builds warn once per machine after 1000 steps. An explicit `options.maxIterations` still errors when exceeded. This port adds no blocking limit. The extension caps active internal watches at 32; that is not a hard bound on external follow-up requests or every queue length.

Version-pinned runtime evidence, relative to upstream `packages/core/src` at the beta.1 tag:

| Contract | Source |
| --- | --- |
| Snapshot commit precedes ordered effects | `createActor.ts:1042–1043`, `createActor.ts:122–131` |
| Ordinary transition selection is reused; eventless selection has no result cache | `stateUtils.ts:2339–2368`, `stateUtils.ts:2984–3018`, `stateUtils.ts:3021–3068` |
| Context patches merge shallowly | `transitionActions.ts:355–360` |
| Scheduler uses injected clock for time, schedule and cancel | `system.ts:720–760` |
| Async disposer aborts; late resolve/reject after stop is ignored | `actors/promise.ts:519–555` |
| Default microstep limit is infinite; development warning at 1001 | `stateUtils.ts:2429`, `stateUtils.ts:2703–2733` |

`tests/fixtures/xstate5-suspension.json` was generated with real v5 actors and the production suspension writer at `7fa41bc`, using synthetic watches only. `tests/xstate-migration.test.ts` checks restoration, phase, state, facts, unchanged wire format and the extension reload boundary. Separate private proofs compare copied live-session suspensions against both runtimes with an inert check port; real conditions never execute.

Fleet installation remains a desk decision after candidate verification and a one-desk canary. Running sessions keep their loaded code until restart. A dependency upgrade is not permission to widen the session lifecycle boundary.

## Active adoption versus a pending ancestor

The stale-predecessor report was a display defect, not incorrect immediate targeting. Read-only session evidence showed each successor binding its immediate predecessor, adopting one recurrence, and delivering further ticks under the same watch id. It then followed an unresolved ancestor carried in that predecessor's payload. Matching mailbox receipts confirmed both immediate adoptions. An inherited binding stamped for a different successor was not selected. The separate report of a predecessor with no quit journal or remaining mailbox artifact has no evidence establishing whether it ever published; absence now does not prove historical nonpublication or identify the loaded version.

At `7fa41bc`, `extensions/pi-until.ts:1562–1671` resumes the immediate payload before following its pending predecessor. The actual defect is `extensions/pi-until.ts:784–809`: active watch rows are followed by an unqualified pending warning and an empty-inventory message, without explaining that adoption already succeeded. A pending ancestor does not suspend adopted watches. Keep valid transitive adoption; do not infer stopped timers from a pending link.

The list renderer now names adopted, active ids and their immediate source separately from an additional predecessor handoff. Empty additional inventory does not mean active watches are absent. Provenance comes only from validated adoption records naming this successor, is display-only, and survives same-session reload. Cancelled watches and a fork's inherited adoptions cannot appear as adopted and active here. No cached tool description, prompt snippet, guidelines, wake packet or restoration contract changes.

`tests/handoff-list.test.ts` pins the production shape with synthetic watches: immediate adoption despite inherited ancestor bindings, ticks while that ancestor waits, explicit missing-record release without stopping the adopted recurrence, same-session provenance across reload, fork isolation, cancellation, and expired watches skipped on late ancestor publication. Baseline comparison: `7fa41bc` already adopts and ticks correctly and skips late-expired work, but lacks bounded missing-record release; `7cfa879` already fixes that release and preserves expiry. Both baselines fail the new list clarity assertion. Neither establishes a fix for the unproved publication case. This patch changes presentation, not ownership, persistence or recovery guarantees.

## Darwin probe diagnostics

The real-OS test at `tests/predecessor.test.ts` incorrectly treated an oversized PID as a clean absent lookup. On Darwin, `ps -o lstart= -p 2147483647` exits 1 with empty stdout and a nonempty diagnostic: `ps: process id too large: 2147483647`. Signal zero reports `ESRCH`, but `src/predecessor.ts:76–101` deliberately does not use it after an ambiguous `ps` failure. It returns unknown, never alive or gone. The same code is in `7cfa879`; this test failure does not imply unsafe canary release. A real exited child's PID gives empty output on both streams, exit 1 and `ESRCH`, and correctly maps to missing. Probe classification is unchanged.

Tests now distinguish invalid Darwin input from a real exited child, and inject permission, missing executable, timeout, malformed output and signal-zero ambiguity. Unknown remains unknown even when signal zero alone would say absent. Live start times remain present. A valid identity observation still controls release through the existing death grace and final claim check.

A recorded unknown identity can keep watching beyond the wait ceiling; this is not the separate missing-record ceiling release. The list and ceiling notice now say that the handoff is stuck, show when the current continuous unknown observation began, and explain the existing recovery step: check whether the predecessor has stopped, without stopping it yourself; once death is visible, release follows automatically. If it stays stuck, tell the desk that maintains pi-until. Do not delete the record or re-arm from the warning. The timestamp is diagnostic state in the current wait, not persisted history, and resets after a conclusive observation, a new link or reload. Publication still wins and clears the warning. No override, probe relaxation, new journal format, daemon or cached prompt change is added.

## Delta review follow-up

The diagnostic identity observer is isolated: an observer exception cannot finish a claim, authorize release or prevent a later publication from being adopted. A throwing-observer regression pins that boundary. The real exited-child test retries once only when the PID has already been reused and the probe reports present; unknown remains a test failure. The stuck packet explicitly forbids stopping the predecessor and names the desk maintaining pi-until rather than one fleet desk. The reload-recovery test now uses a ten-second fixture ceiling and a five-second event assertion: its previous 200 ms ceiling could expire during journal or mailbox fsync, and expiry is not the behavior this test exercises. Production deadlines are unchanged.

The first full Darwin review run failed that reload test in both modes. Parallel mode also outpaced the one-second chain-limit notification assertion and the one-second legacy-ancestor deadline assertion. These are retained as explicit failed verification receipts, not silently retried or counted as passes. The logs show the expected transitions had not completed at the assertions; they do not by themselves isolate which filesystem operation took the time. A subsequent Darwin run passed the reload fixture but exposed the same one-second polling assumption in both pending-restoration transfer tests. Those tests now await the existing `completeHandoff` spy's successful settlement, then assert receipt durability and claim cleanup. Their outer test timeout remains bounded at fifteen seconds. This changes only test synchronization, not publication ordering or the production clock. That run's parallel mode also hit five-second whole-test limits in the interrupted-before-rename claim case and the missing-PID death-grace case. Those two tests are unchanged. Final gate results must be reported separately.

Display-only provenance remains a known limitation. `adoptedWatchSources` can credit a locally armed watch to the predecessor when a shutdown record mixes local watches with an interrupted claimed transfer. Crediting only the first record carrying a transfer id is insufficient when that first record is itself the mixed shutdown snapshot. Fixing it honestly needs an unambiguous adoption-only source, not a first-seen heuristic. This does not grant restoration or re-arm permission: the label applies only to already-active ids under do-not-re-arm. Deferred rather than widening this review-nit commit.

## Current receipt

Version `0.6.0` adds automatic quit-then-relaunch recovery; see the PR receipt in the pi-until repo history. Version `0.4.0` passed 59 tests, `npm run check`, `npm run pack:dry`, a real Pi RPC recurrence, independent review, and GitHub Actions. The installed Git source is `git:github.com/joelhooks/pi-until@main`.
